    {
      "id": "gp5KTg0ZIH_foJRmxBHSz",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 435.9500085443826,
      "y": 6752.272067492182,
      "width": 792,
      "height": 725,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "ad",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 999791765,
      "version": 24,
      "versionNonce": 1480961429,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1777645316833,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "The main difference between Docker Engine and a Docker Runtime is that\nthe engine is a complete platform for building and managing containers,\nwhile the runtime is the specific internal component responsible for\nactually executing them.\n\nDocker Engine:Docker Engine is the top-level \"container engine\" that\ndevelopers interact with. It provides a full suite of management\ntools.Purpose: Manages the entire container lifecycle, including\nbuilding images, networking, and volume storage.\n\nComponents: Includes the \ni)Docker CLI (the commands you type), \nii)the REST API, \niii)and the Docker Daemon (dockerd).\n\nRole: Acts as a high-level manager that takes user commands and\ndelegates the heavy lifting to the runtime.\n\n\nDocker RuntimeThe container runtime is a lower-level component that\nworks \"under the hood\" of the engine.Purpose: Handles the technical\ntasks of starting, running, and stopping individual container processes\non the host operating system.Key Examples:containerd: A \"high-level\"\nruntime that manages images and hands off execution to a lower\nlayer.runc: A \"low-level\" runtime that performs the actual container\ncreation using Linux kernel features.Standardization: Most modern\nruntimes follow Open Container Initiative (OCI) standards, allowing them\nto be swapped out (e.g., using containerd without the full Docker Engine\nin Kubernetes).",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "The main difference between Docker Engine and a Docker Runtime is that the engine is a complete platform for building and managing containers, while the runtime is the specific internal component responsible for actually executing them.\n\nDocker Engine:Docker Engine is the top-level \"container engine\" that developers interact with. It provides a full suite of management tools.Purpose: Manages the entire container lifecycle, including building images, networking, and volume storage.\n\nComponents: Includes the \ni)Docker CLI (the commands you type), \nii)the REST API, \niii)and the Docker Daemon (dockerd).\n\nRole: Acts as a high-level manager that takes user commands and delegates the heavy lifting to the runtime.\n\n\nDocker RuntimeThe container runtime is a lower-level component that works \"under the hood\" of the engine.Purpose: Handles the technical tasks of starting, running, and stopping individual container processes on the host operating system.Key Examples:containerd: A \"high-level\" runtime that manages images and hands off execution to a lower layer.runc: A \"low-level\" runtime that performs the actual container creation using Linux kernel features.Standardization: Most modern runtimes follow Open Container Initiative (OCI) standards, allowing them to be swapped out (e.g., using containerd without the full Docker Engine in Kubernetes).",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
