    {
      "id": "blXKXA5ujdN-S4eYVJJ_N",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 474.2432510417042,
      "y": 10338.109651691084,
      "width": 523.5997924804688,
      "height": 35,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "an",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1402653819,
      "version": 46,
      "versionNonce": 915217723,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1777645744583,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Docker Image vs Docker Containers ",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Docker Image vs Docker Containers ",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "tZhJUIgMs7YwK_9pyXBwk",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 473.10921732914994,
      "y": 10392.359100947167,
      "width": 781,
      "height": 325,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aq",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1363684117,
      "version": 850,
      "versionNonce": 1936624629,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1777646054026,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "A Docker \"image\" behaves like a template from which consistent\ncontainers can be created. If Docker was a traditional virtual machine \n, the image could  linked to the ISO used to install your \nVM. This isn't a robust comparision , as docker differs from VMs\nin terms of both concept and implementation , but its useful starting \npoint nonetheless\n\n\nImages define the initial filesystem state of new containers\n.They bundle our application's source code and its dependencies into\na self contained package that's ready to use with a container \nruntime.Within the image , filesystem content is represented \nas multiple independent layers",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "A Docker \"image\" behaves like a template from which consistent\ncontainers can be created. If Docker was a traditional virtual machine \n, the image could  linked to the ISO used to install your \nVM. This isn't a robust comparision , as docker differs from VMs\nin terms of both concept and implementation , but its useful starting \npoint nonetheless\n\n\nImages define the initial filesystem state of new containers\n.They bundle our application's source code and its dependencies into\na self contained package that's ready to use with a container \nruntime.Within the image , filesystem content is represented \nas multiple independent layers",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "CvSew3udRaxmQrs--6yNd",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 482.9915209979687,
      "y": 10799.477203754126,
      "width": 363,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "ar",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 209310389,
      "version": 41,
      "versionNonce": 1373633301,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1777646721545,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Let's understand container vs VMs",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Let's understand container vs VMs",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "oqNwXcpgfChJ3xW5h0rnn",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 484.9356232868704,
      "y": 10851.480754659533,
      "width": 693,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "as",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 812775893,
      "version": 73,
      "versionNonce": 836602421,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1777646746938,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Before that we need to understand the process of virtualization",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Before that we need to understand the process of virtualization",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "t-b_zMd--U0qwi52uRNRy",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 474.1748825124828,
      "y": 10895.341174713683,
      "width": 781,
      "height": 325,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "at",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 206189787,
      "version": 90,
      "versionNonce": 2048039835,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1777646781657,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "What Is Virtualization?\nVirtualization is the process of creating a virtual version or\nrepresentation of computing resources like servers, storage devices,\noperating systems (OS), or networks that are abstracted from the\nphysical computing hardware. This abstraction enables greater\nflexibility, scalability, and agility in managing and deploying\ncomputing resources. You can create multiple virtual computers from the\nhardware and software components of a single machine. You can think of\nit as essentially a computer-generated computer.\n\n\nNow lets understand hypervisor \n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "What Is Virtualization?\nVirtualization is the process of creating a virtual version or representation of computing resources like servers, storage devices, operating systems (OS), or networks that are abstracted from the physical computing hardware. This abstraction enables greater flexibility, scalability, and agility in managing and deploying computing resources. You can create multiple virtual computers from the hardware and software components of a single machine. You can think of it as essentially a computer-generated computer.\n\n\nNow lets understand hypervisor \n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "_cn6O9UhuxgMwyAEHieAq",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 479.3590168510377,
      "y": 11206.483610552888,
      "width": 781,
      "height": 500,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "au",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 783932795,
      "version": 62,
      "versionNonce": 1749833947,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1777646809814,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "What Is a Hypervisor?\n\nThe software that enables the creation and management of virtual\ncomputing environments is called a hypervisor. It’s a lightweight\nsoftware or firmware layer that sits between the physical hardware and\nthe virtualized environments and allows multiple operating systems to\nrun concurrently on a single physical machine. The hypervisor abstracts\nand partitions the underlying hardware resources, such as central\nprocessing units (CPUs), memory, storage, and networking, and allocates\nthem to the virtual environments.  You can think of the hypervisor as\nthe middleman that pulls resources from the raw materials of your\ninfrastructure and directs them to the various computing instances.\n\nThere are two types of hypervisors: \n\nType 1, bare-metal hypervisors, run directly on the hardware. \nType 2 hypervisors operate within a host operating system. \n\nHypervisors are fundamental to virtualization technology, enabling\nefficient utilization and management of computing resources.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "What Is a Hypervisor?\n\nThe software that enables the creation and management of virtual computing environments is called a hypervisor. It’s a lightweight software or firmware layer that sits between the physical hardware and the virtualized environments and allows multiple operating systems to run concurrently on a single physical machine. The hypervisor abstracts and partitions the underlying hardware resources, such as central processing units (CPUs), memory, storage, and networking, and allocates them to the virtual environments.  You can think of the hypervisor as the middleman that pulls resources from the raw materials of your infrastructure and directs them to the various computing instances.\n\nThere are two types of hypervisors: \n\nType 1, bare-metal hypervisors, run directly on the hardware. \nType 2 hypervisors operate within a host operating system. \n\nHypervisors are fundamental to virtualization technology, enabling efficient utilization and management of computing resources.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
